# Fast and light (absolute bottom line)

OzIntel runs **fast and light**. That is the absolute bottom line of this project. Do not re-litigate it. Do not add anything that makes the app feel slow, heavy, or stuck — especially on phones / PWA restore paths.

## Hard rules

1. **No heavy work on request hot paths.** API handlers for restore, login/session, catalogue, alerts, and similar must not scan every owner silo, merge photo trees, run multi-step migrations, or do bulk “sync everyone” loops.
2. **One job per request.** Read the minimum JSON needed, write the minimum change, return. Prefer `readUsersFast()` (or equivalent) over full bootstrap/migration readers on hot paths.
3. **Migrations are rare and offline.** Seed/migrate/consolidate only on explicit admin/setup/one-shot tools — never on every GET/POST that customers hit.
4. **No speculative complexity.** Do not add background merges, double round-trips, or “just in case” filesystem walks to fix a bug. Fix the direct path.
5. **If speed and a feature conflict, speed wins.** Strip the feature or defer it; do not leave a hang, timeout, or multi-second spinner in production.
6. **Keep the stack thin.** Prefer simple JSON files and small handlers. Do not introduce heavy ORMs, large frameworks, or request-time batch jobs without an explicit user ask that accepts the cost.

## When changing bottle shop / auth / ops

- Restore must answer in about a second: match user or ledger row → set session → return payload (include catalogue in-band when useful).
- Ledger admin load: read one store under the configured owner. Do not consolidate all owners on page load.
- Never reintroduce invite-code complexity or silo-merge-on-restore.

If a change would make OzIntel slower or heavier, do not ship it.
